Step 15 - Install Jenkins
Objective
Install Jenkins on the DevOps Server to automate CI/CD pipelines.

Step 15.1 - Verify Java Installation
Why?
Jenkins is a Java-based application.

Without Java:
Jenkins ❌ Cannot Start
Command
java --version
Expected Output
openjdk version "21.x.x"
Learning
Java Runtime Environment (JRE) is required to run Jenkins.

Step 15.2 - Install Java (If Not Installed)
Command
sudo apt install -y openjdk-21-jdk
Verification
java --version
Learning
OpenJDK provides the Java Runtime and Java Development Kit required by Jenkins.

Step 15.3 - Download Jenkins Repository Key
Why?
Ubuntu must trust the Jenkins repository before installing packages from it.
Command
sudo wget -O /etc/apt/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable/jenkins.io-2026.key
Learning
The Jenkins GPG key is used to verify package authenticity and integrity.

Step 15.4 - Add Jenkins Repository
Why?
Tell Ubuntu where Jenkins packages are located.
Command
echo "deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc] https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable binary/" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/jenkins.list > /dev/null
Learning
A repository is a location from which Ubuntu downloads software packages.

Step 15.5 - Update Package Information
Why?
Refresh package metadata after adding the Jenkins repository.
Command
sudo apt update
Learning
APT updates its package index before installing software.

Step 15.6 - Install Jenkins
Command
sudo apt install -y jenkins
Learning
This installs Jenkins and creates the Jenkins service on the server.

Step 15.7 - Enable Jenkins Service
Why?
Start Jenkins automatically after every server reboot.
Command
sudo systemctl enable jenkins
Learning
enable configures the service to start automatically during system boot.

Step 15.8 - Start Jenkins Service
Command
sudo systemctl start jenkins
Learning
Starts the Jenkins service immediately.

Step 15.9 - Verify Jenkins Status
Command
sudo systemctl status jenkins
Expected Output
active (running)
Learning
Systemctl is used to manage Linux services.

Step 15.10 - Verify Jenkins Port
Command
sudo ss -tulnp | grep 8080
Expected Output
LISTEN
0.0.0.0:8080
Learning
Jenkins listens on port 8080 by default.

Step 15.11 - Access Jenkins UI
Open in browser:
http://13.201.121.131:8080
Expected Screen
Unlock Jenkins
Step 15.12 - Retrieve Initial Admin Password
Command
sudo cat /var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword
Why?
Jenkins generates a one-time password during installation.
Learning
This password is used to unlock Jenkins for the first time.
